$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A seizure traced backwards</h2>
<p>On 1 September 2026, police seized 380 Nitrazepam tablets from one Saravanan. The trail led to a godown at Perundurai, where a far larger stock was recovered — 57,770 Nitrazepam tablets, 8,400 Alprazolam 0.5 mg tablets and 3,600 tablets of a tramadol-based preparation. The petitioner, a salaried employee of a pharmacy, was arrested the next day. The case was registered under Sections 8(c), 22 and 29(1) of the NDPS Act (the charge later altered to Section 22(c)) along with Section 278 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita.</p>

<p>What the police actually seized from the petitioner was a mobile phone. The case against him rested on confession statements of co-accused recorded by the police.</p>

<h2>The Court's reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Confessions barred</strong> — Under Section 23(1) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, confessions made to police officers are not provable against the accused (para 7).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Nothing else connects</strong> — Apart from those statements, no material specifically linked the petitioner to the tablets (para 8).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Employment is not complicity</strong> — Working at the pharmacy, without more, does not connect him to the contraband (para 9).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Section 37 not shown</strong> — The tablets seized from the third accused were not shown to be attributable to the petitioner, so the commercial-quantity bail rigour was not attracted (para 11).</div>
</div>

<p>Justice N. Ramesh drew on a settled line of Supreme Court authority — including <em>Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu</em> (2021) 4 SCC 1 on the inadmissibility of confessional statements to officers, and <em>Union of India v. Mohd. Nawaz Khan</em> (2021) 10 SCC 100 on what connects an accused to contraband — while leaving the conspiracy charge under Section 29 entirely open for trial. Bail followed, on a Rs 25,000 bond with two sureties and reporting conditions before the Judicial Magistrate, Tiruvallur.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>What the prosecution had</th><th>Why it could not sustain custody</th></tr>
<tr><td>Confession statements of co-accused to police</td><td>Barred by Section 23(1) BSA; not provable against the accused</td></tr>
<tr><td>Petitioner's employment at the pharmacy</td><td>Presence on the payroll is not possession or conspiracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>A mobile phone seized from him</td><td>No incriminating link shown at this stage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large seizure from a co-accused's godown</td><td>Not attributable to the petitioner, so Section 37 not engaged</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For defence practitioners:</strong> in multi-accused NDPS cases, the first question at the bail stage is what evidence exists against this accused, stripped of co-accused police statements. If the answer is employment, proximity or a phone, the Section 37 threshold may never be reached — and the general bail principles, rather than the NDPS rigour, govern. The new Sakshya Adhiniyam has not diluted the old protections; Section 23 carries them forward intact.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The order is a bail ruling, not an acquittal: the conspiracy allegation remains for trial, and breach of conditions can undo the liberty granted. But it reaffirms, under the new evidence code, a principle older than the NDPS Act itself — the police cannot build a case on confessions made to the police.</p>
</div>
